FINDINGS:
1. [BLOCKER] src/runtime.rs:1728 — The new fractional `time.unix` answer is floor-based (`timestamp()` floors, then a positive fraction is added), but both numeric consumers read it with `as i64` (truncate toward zero) at src/runtime.rs:581, so a pre-epoch fraction names the wrong second: `time.unix(time.parse("1969-12-31T23:59:59.5Z"))` is `-0.5`, while `time.format(-0.5)` and `time.from_unix(-0.5)` both land on second 0 (1970-01-01), one second after the date the text named. This contradicts SPEC.md:1427 ("`time.from_unix(seconds)` is the inverse"), SURVEY.md:271, and REPORT.md:44-48 ("the timestamp, the date value and `time.format` name the same instant"); the existing truncation test only pins `time.from_unix(-1.9)`, not a value emitted by `time.unix`. — Fix: either read numeric instants with `floor()` in `seconds_for_instant` and update the truncation test/spec, or stop emitting negative fractional answers and document that they are not round-trippable; add a test for `time.from_unix(time.unix(<pre-epoch fractional date>))`. — VERIFIED: traced `parse_iso8601` to NaiveDateTime 1969-12-31 23:59:59.5; `timestamp()` floors to -1 (as the new comment at src/runtime.rs:1725-1727 states); src/runtime.rs:1728 adds 0.5 to get -0.5; src/runtime.rs:581 (`Ok(timestamp as i64)`) truncates -0.5 to 0, and src/runtime.rs:1747 uses the same function for `time.from_unix`.
2. [MAJOR] src/runtime.rs:1728 — Adding the fraction to a large whole-second `f64` rounds up across a second boundary when the fraction is within half an ulp of 1 (ulp is ~2.4e-7 at 1.7e9): `time.unix(time.parse("2024-02-29T23:59:58.999999999Z"))` computes `1709251198.0 + 0.999999999` and yields `1709251199`, so `time.format` names 23:59:59 while the date value is 23:59:58.999999999. At `9999-12-31T23:59:59.999999999Z` it rounds to `253402300800`, which passes the guard at src/runtime.rs:968 (the guard checks the date's whole second, not the rounded result) and formats as `+10000-01-01`. The new tests use only binary-exact .5/.25/.75, so this near-boundary branch is untested. — Fix: clamp the result so it is strictly below `seconds + 1` (e.g. `next_down` when the addition rounds up), or refuse/document fractions not representable at that magnitude, and add a `.999999999` test at both an ordinary date and the window edge. — Traced the f64 arithmetic; not run in this read-only session.
3. [MAJOR] src/runtime.rs:799 — The new shared parser is not the "superset" of the old `time.unix` layout that REPORT.md:41-44 and REPORT.md:110-118 claim: it rejects a leap second (`second > 59`) while the old `time.unix` went through chrono's `%S`, which accepts 00-60, so `time.unix("2016-12-31 23:59:60")` used to answer a number and now raises; and `date_digits` (src/runtime.rs:606, called with count 2 at 722/734/767/779/798) requires exactly two digits, whereas chrono's `%m/%d/%H/%M/%S` accept one or two, so `time.unix("2024-1-1 0:0:0")` is likely another regression. SPEC.md:1545 does document the leap-second refusal, so the code may be intended, but the compatibility claim is false and no regression test covers either input. — Fix: either preserve the old surface for `time.unix` (accept 1-2 digit fields and leap seconds, or keep a separate old-layout parser), or correct the REPORT/SPEC claim and add explicit regression tests. — Could not run here; settle by running both inputs against the previous commit.
4. [MINOR] SURVEY.md:194 — The survey's evidence citations were not re-anchored after this phase's ~350-line parser insert and stdlib additions: `src/runtime.rs:815` and `:812` (SURVEY.md:194/243) now point into the fraction parser, but `length` is at src/runtime.rs:1470 and its character count at :1494; `src/runtime.rs:1668` (SURVEY.md:269) is inside `time_sleep`, while the `time_format` arm is at :1676; and `src/stdlib.rs:929` (SURVEY.md:196) should now be :1250. REPORT.md:14 claims the citations were re-anchored. — Fix: update those citations to the current lines. 
5. [MINOR] src/runtime.rs:744 — An out-of-range but well-formed year is reported at the day field: `time.parse("999999-01-01")` passes `date_year` and the field ranges, then `from_ymd_opt` returns None and the error says "expected a day that exists in that month, found `01`" at position 10, blaming the day for a year chrono cannot represent. — Fix: check the year/date range separately and name the year in the error; add a test for a year beyond chrono's maximum.

